WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.140
Hi good humans! My name is Ragini and I'm the director of this episode of Spectrum.

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:09.540
We're very excited to be partnering with AARP again, especially during the special month of May

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:12.239
which is also the Asian Pacific American Heritage Month.

00:00:12.309 --> 00:00:16.948
We're celebrating this month-long celebration with AARP the nation's largest nonprofit

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:21.000
organization working to improve the lives of 50+ and their families.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.789
We absolutely love AARP and their mission to empower people to choose how they live as they age

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:32.399
In fact, we previously partnered with them for another episode of Spectrum, "Do all Asian American Parents Think the Same?".

00:00:32.559 --> 00:00:36.419
So if we haven't seen that make sure to check it out, but for now enjoy this episode!

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:47.500
Hi everyone, my name is Michelle. I'm studying chemical engineering right now. I'm a Cambodian American woman.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:49.560
My name is Julie. I was born in Korea.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:54.540
And I've been in the States ever since I was four. I teach Asian American Studies at Cal State Long Beach

00:00:54.780 --> 00:01:00.560
My name is Ayesha. I was born in Pakistan and I run a used car dealership.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:07.400
My name is Marjana. I was born in Bangladesh and I currently work as an associate at a financial services firm

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:13.380
My name is Luguma I am an undergraduate student and I am Tibetan American.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:14.400
My name is Lillian

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:20.060
I'm going to be graduating with my undergrad in neuroscience pre-medicine. My ethnicity is Hmong American.

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:31.380
3, 2 ,1, go.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:41.999
Yeah, I think people look at me and expect me to have soft opinions in my kind of assertion

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:49.559
I can tell that they did expect me to play into that stereotype and they're surprised that I didn't and then they quickly then

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:53.460
Shift from "okay she's not a lotus flower. She's a dragon lady, got it."

00:01:53.619 --> 00:01:54.119
Yeah

00:01:54.119 --> 00:02:01.258
I actually had the same experience being kind of viewed as a weak character and that consequently made me more

00:02:01.689 --> 00:02:07.319
Hot-headed because I felt like I had to prove the opposite. So in middle school like late middle school early high school

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:11.339
I was really rowdy and I would, you know, like argue with people all the time

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:14.920
But I'm fortunate that within my own family

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:19.480
especially with my father, he really stood up for me.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:23.829
I remember one time when I was sitting at the dinner table and

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:29.260
We had guests over for dinner and one older man was like, okay like you're dismissed

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:31.180
You can go now and my dad was like no

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:37.540
I want her to stay like we're having a conversation and he let me kind of contribute and debate with them and I think

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:40.720
That's always been a really important experience for me.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:42.720
My cousin in Pakistan

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:44.420
She is the perfect

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:51.459
docile woman and she is very quiet and sweet and it almost sounds like she's whispering all the time and

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:55.569
Everyone in my family praises her and says that she's the perfect girl

00:02:55.570 --> 00:03:02.379
I would sit with my legs far apart on purpose just to like piss off family members because I

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:05.340
hated to being told to be

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:09.980
Soft-spoken and bring out the chai and serve and be submissive.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:11.500
From my experience,

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:19.240
I think I've kind of like played into it a little bit but like in the home rather or like with people of

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:22.630
authority and I think a lot of the way I act is

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:27.700
like in striving for acceptance, but like in society like I'm

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:31.660
Kind of I like put on my other hat and kind of headstrong

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:36.490
I don't want to conform to whatever you're putting me in but then for my family like I want you to love me

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:40.380
So I'm going to like be that daughter for you.

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:45.580
Three, two, one, go.

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:53.200
I was very fortunate to have a mom who was very lenient and

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:58.930
respectful, and I think that actually allowed for me to to kind of like open up more and

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:02.019
actually explore things that I liked and didn't like and

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:05.350
Naturally allowed me to create boundaries for myself.

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:13.029
I think that there's kind of like degrees to the tiger mom. With my mom, personally, when I was younger growing up

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:15.309
I remember her being pretty strict

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:18.100
Which I've grown to appreciate now that I'm older.

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:24.669
Once I start to be able to form my own opinions and be more mature, she became more lenient and kind of like a friend

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:29.259
I think Tiger mom's kind of get a bad rap. I also think there's a lot of misunderstanding

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:32.649
around them. I think Tiger moms come from

00:04:33.289 --> 00:04:38.889
This kind of like Asian collective idea that our lives are all linked together, right?

00:04:38.889 --> 00:04:42.908
So if you fail, we all fail. Personally, I was like a latchkey kid

00:04:43.039 --> 00:04:48.789
So my parents were always working so I didn't have people telling me like you need to do your homework

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:50.710
You shouldn't be meeting these kids

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:55.870
I had to learn that all on my own and then eventually I was like a high school dropout

00:04:55.870 --> 00:05:00.250
So I think Tiger moms like their biggest strength is presence, there's there

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:06.250
I actually completely agree with that and my parents were definitely tiger parents. That's what I grew up with

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:09.729
You know there there is undue pressure on the child

00:05:09.729 --> 00:05:15.639
I still feel that today because I live with my parents. That being said I do appreciate it

00:05:15.639 --> 00:05:19.778
I feel like I am the person that I am and I like who I am because of them

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:21.640
yeah.

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:29.780
3, 2, 1, go.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:34.560
Two years ago

00:05:34.569 --> 00:05:36.569
My mom was driving me back up to college

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:41.589
and like helping me move in that year and for like maybe ten minutes away from campus and she goes,

00:05:42.349 --> 00:05:47.829
"Lilian have you found any nice, like Hmong boys there at school?" and I was like, "no mom

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:51.849
I haven't found any," and she's like, "oh, what about any good Christian boys," and I was like "no

00:05:51.849 --> 00:05:55.449
I haven't found any of those either" and she's like, "uh, you know, why?"

00:05:55.969 --> 00:05:59.199
"It's because you're so bossy. You're so impatient"

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:00.189
Yada, yada yada

00:06:00.189 --> 00:06:04.569
like these are the reasons why you're still single and like you need to be a good like

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:07.839
You need to prepare yourself to become a good future wife

00:06:07.969 --> 00:06:12.879
And she just continued this conversation about like how she didn't want to be the mom back at

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:18.520
Like back at home, at our church, but then our families in our communities to be like, yeah

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:21.400
My daughter's the last one out of everyone to get married.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:22.820
How old were you again, Lillian?

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:25.180
So it's just two years ago, so I was 20.

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:28.960
Oh my god, you have so much time. You're still so young. It's okay

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:35.620
In my family, marriage is a very big thing and it also comes from just cultural

00:06:36.229 --> 00:06:40.749
Aspects but also because my parents are Muslim. I live in a Muslim family

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:47.709
and so when I was younger they stressed education and they stressed like doing well and all of these other things but

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:51.550
Outside of that like the entire Bengali community would come up to me

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:54.849
they're like oh but don't work too hard because you have to be

00:06:55.099 --> 00:07:02.259
Marketable for marriage or things like that and my parents would never say that but there was also the lingering thought that like at

00:07:02.539 --> 00:07:04.900
21 that I would get an arranged marriage.

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:09.489
Asian women, growing up for me, were pure innocent virgins and

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:16.659
I never had a boyfriend in high school never kissed a boy. So I was very oppressed  in that way

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:22.419
so when I was 18, I eloped and got married and

00:07:23.569 --> 00:07:25.130
I blame a lot of that on my

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:31.120
hormones because I just couldn't wait any longer and the only way for me to get it was to get married and

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:38.460
I look back, and I think did I marry too young? I chose well because we're still married today

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:44.260
it's just those pressures of being an Asian woman where if I was allowed to

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:50.100
Be myself, then. I don't think I would have gotten married that young. We would have waited.

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:57.000
My mom married my dad at a very young age. Growing up, they've really instilled values of

00:07:58.219 --> 00:08:03.699
Establishing yourself first, like getting an education, making sure that you go after what you want in life

00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:08.889
And in fact, like they actually want me to delay it they're like don't even think about boys yet

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:11.349
You know you want to be a successful engineer

00:08:12.229 --> 00:08:18.008
go get your job and go, you know, like establish yourself in a professional sense and

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:20.539
personal well-being

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:28.810
Three, two, one, go.

00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:35.020
I think my parents' expectations

00:08:35.270 --> 00:08:37.359
Versus what's gonna happen is a little different

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:41.800
They always tell me, you know, once you're married and you have a family they are your priority

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:46.779
You can't be taking care of us, but I I already know that there is still

00:08:47.570 --> 00:08:54.809
This unstated expectation, this more universal expectation beyond my parents for me to take care of my parents financially,

00:08:55.720 --> 00:09:01.919
physically when the time comes but I think my parents as individuals they don't they don't expect that from me

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:08.160
My parents have shared that with me too that like when I get married I'm, you know, I shouldn't be worrying about them

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:13.649
But I'm in a new family now and I think my answer to this question would be entirely different if I was a man

00:09:13.870 --> 00:09:20.190
Because what the Hmong culture it's that when you get married you leave your family and you join your husband's side of the family

00:09:20.190 --> 00:09:23.460
I see that with my older sister who recently got married, you know my mom

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:26.819
So it's just pushing her to like do things for her new family

00:09:27.910 --> 00:09:34.829
But that's not going to be the case right because you know, obviously when a girl gets married she doesn't forego her old family

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:36.760
It's still family

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:37.740
Well, my parents speak English

00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:45.200
But they have slight accents and they don't understand when somebody's being sarcastic with them or there's like a racial

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:47.520
microaggression. Growing up

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:49.060
I used to read important

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:54.179
Documents for my parents and try to translate them just so that they don't get in trouble with the government

00:09:54.190 --> 00:09:57.809
And so I feel this need to almost protect them also

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:03.210
Do you think I sell cars because I like cars. No, my dad started the family business

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:06.660
Just seeing them so vulnerable in this country. I just took it upon myself

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:09.570
I did it because I wanted to be a good daughter

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:17.940
On both sides of my family on my mom's and my dad's side all of the children are very much involved in taking care of

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:19.940
the aging parents lives

00:10:19.950 --> 00:10:23.489
It's really about just collectively coming together and taking care of someone who

00:10:23.890 --> 00:10:29.160
dedicated their whole lives to taking care of you and just giving back but with my parents at least they

00:10:29.710 --> 00:10:35.129
Don't want me to take care of them. Their only rule is that I don't put them in a nursing home

00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:38.699
They said if anything find a really good caretaker

00:10:38.980 --> 00:10:41.280
But just like don't kick us

00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:45.419
You know, away and forget about us or like mistreat us

00:10:52.120 --> 00:10:54.929
Three, two, one, go

00:11:00.100 --> 00:11:01.690
Hell no

00:11:01.690 --> 00:11:04.900
I never feel like I have the right to be sad or upset

00:11:04.900 --> 00:11:09.700
I remember telling my dad for the first time that I was depressed and he just looked at me and laughed

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:16.470
just like, "what do you have to be depressed about?" And it's hard to explain to a man who

00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:19.709
Literally started working when he was 12 years old

00:11:19.930 --> 00:11:26.459
A man who had to say goodbye to his mom and dad and immigrated to this country and then like a few months later

00:11:26.459 --> 00:11:29.219
They died. So it's like I

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:31.540
I just like sweep it under the rug

00:11:31.540 --> 00:11:36.599
I think that there is such a stigma associated with mental health and

00:11:36.940 --> 00:11:43.559
and that's why it's so difficult in my family to talk about it because I think in for Bengalis in general

00:11:44.230 --> 00:11:45.610
mental illness

00:11:45.610 --> 00:11:49.860
Equates to crazy like just being crazy and like for my parents

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:53.099
It's like you're either crazy or you're not, right?

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:59.100
But I remember the first time that I really realized that I was depressed

00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:04.440
it was when I was in college and I attended Columbia and I remember just being very

00:12:04.630 --> 00:12:07.829
Upset and I couldn't tell that to my parents, but I just told them like, you know

00:12:07.829 --> 00:12:12.599
I really didn't want to feel this way or go to the school and I remember my mom saying

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:16.680
Well, at least you got to go and I was like, oh well

00:12:17.529 --> 00:12:21.779
Now I can't feel bad because like you never had that opportunity

00:12:22.089 --> 00:12:29.669
so like I think a lot of mental illness a lot of what we feel now is very different from our

00:12:30.010 --> 00:12:31.240
parents'

00:12:31.240 --> 00:12:35.130
Experiences and so they can't necessarily like relate to it

00:12:35.350 --> 00:12:39.690
My story's a little different because I actually had depression when I was seven

00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:46.499
And so I was a little kid and I was lucky enough to have parents who took me to a clinic to get help

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:49.529
I know within the Hmong community. We are very spiritual people

00:12:49.779 --> 00:12:54.629
So if you do have a mental health illness. Oh, it's not like other factors. It's like your spirit

00:12:54.779 --> 00:12:56.520
It's your soul. Something's wrong with your soul

00:12:56.520 --> 00:13:03.179
um, you both talked about having guilts, like knowing that your parents went through so much and you know,

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:08.400
You're here living like a life better than they could, but you're still feeling down. You still feeling depressed

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:13.629
You still have anxiety and I think a big thing of that is like generational. There's such thing as like generational

00:13:14.959 --> 00:13:18.720
Depression, mental health it impacts you all that.

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:21.879
I completely agree with you about the kind of the

00:13:22.399 --> 00:13:26.889
Cultural gap and the generational gap. I think also in my parents language

00:13:26.959 --> 00:13:31.659
They just grew up not even hearing about such a thing as mental health

00:13:31.660 --> 00:13:38.260
I can talk to my family if I'm not feeling well. I can talk about the problem, but I think I do

00:13:39.260 --> 00:13:45.249
Not directly use words like, "I don't feel mentally well" or, "I feel depressed"

00:13:46.070 --> 00:13:52.839
because I think there is such a stigma attached to those words in the larger Asian community and in my community

00:13:56.450 --> 00:14:00.309
3, 2, 1, go

00:14:04.910 --> 00:14:08.350
It's kind of funny how like Asian people

00:14:09.140 --> 00:14:13.689
Like can be used as kind of a prop to further like people's agendas

00:14:13.690 --> 00:14:17.260
like we can be seen as a model minority and like, ok, why aren't other

00:14:17.839 --> 00:14:23.529
Ethnicities or racial groups, you know like performing as well as them but now it's like ok

00:14:23.529 --> 00:14:28.929
We're kind of cast in a light where we're like the reason for you know

00:14:28.930 --> 00:14:35.560
This current issue that we're all facing globally like it's your fault and that's why a lot of people are being like physically

00:14:36.140 --> 00:14:38.649
Like attacked it feels a little

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:41.320
isolating there's like

00:14:41.750 --> 00:14:46.929
Not many people who are allies for us, but they're just like, okay. Yeah, don't target people because they're Asian

00:14:47.480 --> 00:14:50.980
but like I still feel at home because I

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:56.400
Grew up here. I live here and this is home for me.

00:14:56.520 --> 00:14:57.700
I actually feel very

00:14:58.040 --> 00:15:02.400
differently. So I was born and raised in the US lived there for 20 years and

00:15:03.589 --> 00:15:09.819
Just now in August, this past August, moved to Tokyo. I feel much more at home in Asia

00:15:10.130 --> 00:15:14.739
Actually than I do in America. I think it's just been all my

00:15:15.380 --> 00:15:16.640
experiences kind of

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:23.199
Accumulated growing up and I don't really miss America much surprisingly and that really surprised me because that's where I grew up

00:15:23.200 --> 00:15:25.440
It's my native language, it's my native home

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:28.880
So I was raised both here in the United States and in South Asia

00:15:28.960 --> 00:15:34.360
And I've never felt a hundred percent like when I'm in Pakistan, I'm the American

00:15:34.370 --> 00:15:39.529
Then when I'm here I'm the Pakistani, and so neither place has treated me

00:15:39.529 --> 00:15:44.628
as like, I'm a hundred percent I belong there and that's my home and I've come to terms, you know

00:15:44.629 --> 00:15:45.560
It's never gonna be like that

00:15:45.560 --> 00:15:47.100
But it's okay

00:15:47.100 --> 00:15:52.500
This entire time that I've been here like South Asians Muslim Americans. We have been ostracized and

00:15:52.700 --> 00:15:57.580
Now I'm saying there is this shift it's going to East Asians and my heart goes out to you guys

00:15:57.589 --> 00:15:59.958
But it might never really go away

00:16:00.480 --> 00:16:04.430
Once you get that hit, it kind of stays with your community

00:16:04.430 --> 00:16:11.329
I was actually just going to say that I mean I am NOT personally affected like I'm not part of one of the ethnic groups

00:16:11.329 --> 00:16:14.659
That's been harmed or targeted because of COVID-19

00:16:15.509 --> 00:16:17.509
but speaking to

00:16:17.790 --> 00:16:23.689
9/11 and the backlash from that I don't see that going away and I feel like

00:16:24.060 --> 00:16:27.560
This may not go away either and it's unfortunate that

00:16:28.139 --> 00:16:30.139
Minorities aren't allies

00:16:30.389 --> 00:16:36.859
always and they should be because in times like these we need people to speak up for us when we

00:16:37.230 --> 00:16:39.740
Don't have a voice or feel threatened

00:16:39.740 --> 00:16:46.309
I think right now we kind of have this flourishing of Asian American storytelling, right?

00:16:47.020 --> 00:16:49.940
There's affirmative action which by the way, absolutely

00:16:49.940 --> 00:16:51.560
benefits Asian-Americans, too

00:16:51.569 --> 00:16:55.128
So we see Asian-Americans in these different spaces that we hadn't seen them before

00:16:55.439 --> 00:17:01.519
But I think the Yellow Peril idea never really went away that it is what Asian Americans still are

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:05.569
And it's just we can put as much paint over it as we want

00:17:05.569 --> 00:17:10.069
But all it takes is one virus and then that comes right back up

00:17:10.380 --> 00:17:14.089
You know a lot of Asian Americans are like we need other people to stand up with us

00:17:14.089 --> 00:17:19.999
We need other people of color to condemn this but I'm like we didn't really step up for these other people either

00:17:20.360 --> 00:17:25.370
I can't expect, we can't expect other people of color to care

00:17:25.709 --> 00:17:31.438
When they remember when they were going through something similar and we were like, "Sorry, I'm the model minority"

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:36.720
I can help you. You know. I just I think there's such a trauma within each of our groups

00:17:37.239 --> 00:17:43.409
and we don't realize that we'd be so much stronger if we could just see how similar we are

00:17:43.840 --> 00:17:47.129
And also, despite 9/11, the reason why I selected agree

00:17:47.129 --> 00:17:53.579
It's because I still feel like I'm entitled to share the same spaces of other people so I still consider the U.S.

00:17:53.580 --> 00:18:00.779
My home, I feel like I belong here and no one could really take that away from me. Even if they try.

00:18:01.299 --> 00:18:04.858
I wish I could give you all a hug right now. It's really nice to

00:18:05.559 --> 00:18:10.858
Know that other people like share similar experiences and to like learn about your backgrounds as well. Yeah

00:18:10.859 --> 00:18:15.659
This has been really enlightening just talking to people from all over with a lot of different backgrounds

00:18:15.659 --> 00:18:19.469
So thank you for achieving such a diverse cohort. It's been really nice

00:18:20.139 --> 00:18:27.149
